A||||10||Homicidal||Oil treaments, octane boosters, and others.  Are they worth it???||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||07-31-2000||04:31 PM||||I am not a mechanic, i cannot drop the cradle out and prevent aspring problems.  But I DO like to keep my car running good by changing the oil, plugs, and keeping it lubricated.<P>I see a lot of oil treatments, engine treatments, octane boosters, fuel injector cleaners, etc.. that claim they clean the engine, help it run better, and sustain it's life.<P>While I'm sure a lot of it isn't true, do these products help at ALL in the maitenence of a car?  Should I spend my money on any of them?  If so, which are the best ones to invest in?  If not, why not?  <P>[edit] Also, what is the best weight oil to put in an 84' four-banger?<p>[This message has been edited by Homicidal (edited 07-31-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||07-31-2000||05:16 PM||||I imagine you'll get a variety of responses...everything from "oh yes, I notice a drastic change" to "its all crap, dont waste your time".<BR>Personally, I use fuel injector cleaners/general gas additives from time to time, but not very often. Usually if I by a Fiero that has sat around for a year or two, I'll dump in some sort of gas treatment along with 93 octane gas (which is the only gas I use anyway)....to be honest, I really dont know if it does much, but its usually so cheap that I figure "why not".<BR>The only things I stay away from are these "stop leaks" products for oil, radiator, ect. These things (from what I understand) typically gum up your engine. <BR>I guess its really a "to each his own", they may work, then again, it might be the ole' sugar pill scenerio.............  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||07-31-2000||08:07 PM||||Just about every engine oil additive has been found to be snakeoil crap, so don't waste your time and money. Octane booster is only needed when you're running high compression and can't find high octane gasoline. A good quality fuel injector cleaner is probably not a bad idea once or twice a year.||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||08-01-2000||03:09 AM||||I talked to Homicidal on the phone last night about the headlight motor he needs. We started talking about additives and the irony is that Batboy's response is just about verbatim with what I told Homicidal. Can you say P-L-A-C-E-B-O??? I'm a maniac on oil changes, as I change mine every 1,000-1,500 and use Castrol religiously, but all that synthetic crap, or especially that "one-time" engine oil treatment that's supposed to permeate the metalic poures and increase engine life 'X' amount are a joke. And for $5 spark plugs, which I put in the same class as those snake oils, I say 'show me some data from a large sample size. Not projected rhetoric. There's no free lunch, just preventative maintenance.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||08-01-2000||06:59 AM||||Ed, you are a maniac. No way would you be changing oil that often if you were racking up 500 highway miles a week like I do. I change oil at 5,000 miles intervals. My Suburban has 270k miles on it and the engine's never been apart. Heavy city driving or operation in high dust areas (like driving on gravel roads a lot) does require more frequent changes. This subject has been discussed thoroughly several times in the past. Check out the search function. Here is an interesting link.<P> <A HREF="http://www.jps.net/gtracer/snakeoil.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jps.net/gtracer/snakeoil.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||08-01-2000||01:26 PM||||I use an injector cleaner 2 or 3 times a year too. Only thing ive noticed that DOES work was changing from Valvoline regular oil to Castrol Full Synthetic. I went from using a quart of oil ever 500 miles or so to using none at all. BTW my7Fiero, why do u use 93 oct, do you have a detonation problem. Ive used 87 in everything for years with no problems. my modified Fiero, Lincoln, and Mercedes AMG SL. I hope you do know that 'premium gas' dont mean better gas, just more expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||08-01-2000||02:04 PM||||There are some good fuel injector cleaners out there, but other than that, about the only additive that's any good is STP, and only then in a pretty trashed motor.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||08-01-2000||03:01 PM||||Some guys I know said their motor was almost at it's end, and they used MolySlip (or something liek that) and it helped quite a bit? Anyone ever try this stuff?||||reg||1
Z||000008||BellaFiero||08-01-2000||03:27 PM||||Sorry, but Premium does mean better gas. For my car, at any rate. I have NO knocks or pings with Premium, whereas with anything less it sounds like a rock is pinging around in the back  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||1FST2M6||08-01-2000||03:38 PM||||I used synthetic oil the almost 2 years i had my SE. I went throught 16+ bottles of nitrous and changed the oil probly 6 times in the 10k i had the car. Funny thing is... more than 4 months after i took the nitrous off the car, even with the crazy oil changes and over-priced oil it still grabed a hold of a bearing and riped it to shreads and scratched the crank a bit. all the lubrication and gadatry you throw at a car your still gunna have failure unless you drive nice an sane all the time... by the time i'm driving "nice and sane" i'll be in Florida with all the rest of the slow drivin' blue hairs. and i won't care about oil additives unless they make my arthritis go away. The additive are a good mask for a problem but, so is saw dust in a diff or tranny to cover up a noise... <P>oh as for premium gas.. thats all i'll use... the extra filtration can't hurt and the octane content will keep my toy on the road and putzin' along where the lack of filtration and parafin in the lesser fuels can clog fuel system, cause premature pump failure and you don't get a free car wash with a purchase of $10 or more...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||08-02-2000||07:11 AM||||sorry again. Premium may be 'better' for each of your cars if its prone to detonation or pinging - but- its not better grade perse'. Its all the same exact stuff only the premium has about 5 cents worth of additives that surpresses the detonation and there charging you 25 to 50 cents more per gallon. All im saying here guys is if your not having a problem using reg 87, there is no reason to pay the extra - its just as good. You could put 110 octane in a Honda too and it would work fine if you enjoyed paying $3.00 a gallon but it wouldnt run any better at all. My Fiero gets about 25K per year, lincoln has 250K, and the Benz has going on 300K all on that bad 87 oct.||||reg||1
